  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nCurrent 500 mb analysis shows the center of the mid/upper level low \nover NW Iowa and SW Minnesota. The surface cyclone is currently \ncentered over SE Minnesota and the associated cold front has now \nmoved through most of Missouri, taking the showers with it. \nHowever, air remains saturated near the surface supporting patchy \ndrizzle through the early morning, but conditions will start to dry \nout as WNW flow continues to bring cooler, drier air to the region. \nClouds will stick around for most of the day but will gradually \nclear out from southwest to northeast tonight as the surface low \ncontinues to move NE, except for areas north of US 36. Highs today \nwill feel much more like fall with values in the mid to upper 50s \nand breezy westerly winds.\n\nNear normal temperatures are likely to stick around through the end \nof the week with highs in the the mid to upper 50s and overnight \nlows in the 40s. The 500 mb low over the upper Midwest will progress \nslightly to the NE as another 500 mb low moves into the southern \nplains by Thursday night, resulting in a positively tilted trough \nacross the central US. However, moisture transport will be weak and \nair near the surface will be relatively dry, keeping the chance of \nprecipitation low (<20%). The trough will continue to move east as high \npressure builds in behind it Friday night into Saturday which will \nbring lows for Saturday morning into the low to mid 30s, especially \nfor north central MO but ensemble guidance has <20% chance that \ntemperatures will drop below 32 degrees. Another warming trend is \nlikely early next week with highs returning to the mid to upper 60s \nwhile dry conditions should persist.",
